The rest of this paper is structured as follows: in Section 1.1 we intro duce
notation and deﬁnitions that will be used throughout the paper. In Section 2 we
propose a linear time algorithm – based on a recursion relation – for co mputing
the number of cycles of length g in which an edge is contained, given a graph of
girth g. Next, in Section 3 we propose an algorithm to exhaustively generat e all
egr(v, k, g, λ ) graphs, which uses the algorithm from Section 2 as an important
ingredient. In Section 4 we use these algorithms in a large-scale comp utation
amounting to 6 CPU-years in order to improve several lower and upp er bounds
on n(k, g, λ ) and independently verify several claims using diﬀerent algorithms.
We also highlight several interesting graphs that we discovered and brieﬂy discuss
their properties. Among others, we discuss two graphs that dispr ove a conjecture
by Araujo-Pardo and Leemans [1] for the cases of cubic graphs w ith girths 8 and
12. Finally, in Section 5 we conclude this paper by discussing possible av enues
for further research and we conjecture that n(3, 7, 6) = n(3, 8, 10) = n(3, 8, 12) =
n(3, 8, 14) = ∞ based on our computations.
1.1 Notation and deﬁnitions
For an integer n ≥ 1 we denote by [ n] the set {1, . . . , n }. The girth g of a graph G
is the length of the shortest cycle of G (or ∞ if G is acyclic). A girth cycle of G is
a cycle with length g. We deﬁne ngc(G, u 1u2) as the number of pairwise distinct
girth cycles in G containing the edge u1u2. An edge-girth-regular (v, k, g, λ ) graph
G is a k-regular graph of girth g and order v such that for every edge u1u2, we
have ngc(G, u 1u2) = λ. Such a graph will be abbreviated as an egr(v, k, g, λ )
graph. We deﬁne n(k, g, λ ) as the smallest value of v for which an egr(v, k, g, λ )
graph exists (and deﬁne it as ∞ if no such graph exists). We deﬁne nsp(G, u 1, u 2)
and d(G, u 1, u 2) as, respectively, the number of pairwise distinct shortest paths
and the distance in G between vertices u1 and u2. For an edge u1u2 from the
graph G = ( V, E ), we use G − u1u2 to denote the graph ( V, E \ {u1u2}). The
set NG(u) denotes the neighbors of u in the graph G and degG(u) = |NG(u)|
denotes the degree of u in the graph G.
2 An algorithm for calculating ngc(G, u 1u2)
Let G = ( V, E ) be a graph and u1u2 be an edge of G. We are interested in
obtaining an eﬃcient algorithm for calculating ngc(G, u 1u2), assuming that the

PHYSICAL PAGE 4
4 J. Goedgebeur and J. Jooken
girth g is known (this is indeed the case for the application that we have in mind
in Section 3). The most straightforward idea would consist of trying to eﬃciently
generate all girth cycles of G which contain u1u2 and then counting. However,
the number of girth cycles can be exponential in terms of g so this cannot be
done eﬃciently in general. Instead, in this section we show that ngc(G, u 1u2)
can be calculated with a time complexity of O(|V | + |E|) based on a recursion
relation that does not involve enumerating girth cycles.
We ﬁrst observe a relationship between girth cycles and shortest p aths.
Observation 1 Let u1u2 be an edge of the graph G = (V, E ) with girth g. Now
ngc(G, u 1u2) = 0 if d(G − u1u2, u 1, u 2) > g − 1 and ngc(G, u 1u2) = nsp(G −
u1u2, u 1, u 2) otherwise.
Hence, we can instead focus on computing d(G − u1u2, u 1, u 2) and nsp(G −
u1u2, u 1, u 2). Calculating the distance between two vertices is a classical proble m
that can be solved using breadth-ﬁrst search and a small modiﬁcat ion allows us
to compute the number of shortest paths as well. This becomes clea r in the
following observation.
Observation 2 Let Di = {u ∈ V (G) | d(G − u1u2, u, u 1) = i} be the set of
vertices at distance i from u1 in the graph G − u1u2. We now have the following
base case (for u = u1):
nsp(G − u1u2, u 1, u 1) = 1 .
For a vertex u ̸= u1, let d(G − u1u2, u, u 1) = i > 0. Since every shortest path of
length i can be obtained by adding an edge to a shortest path of length i − 1, we
have the following recursive case:
nsp(G − u1u2, u, u 1) =
∑
u′∈NG−u1u2 (u)∩Di−1
nsp(G − u1u2, u ′, u 1).
These recursion relations can be used to calculate ngc(G, u 1, u 2), assuming
that the girth g is known, with a time complexity of O(|V |+|E|) using a breadth-
ﬁrst search algorithm (the pseudocode is given in Algorithm 1).
3 An algorithm for exhaustively generating all
egr(v, k, g, λ ) graphs
In this section, we describe an algorithm for eﬃciently generating all egr(v, k, g, λ )
graphs for given parameters v, k, g and λ. This algorithm shares similarities with
the algorithms described in [16] and [11] for generating cages. Howe ver, the cur-
rent algorithm requires further structural insights related to ed ge-girth-regular
graphs. We ﬁrst need a lemma that tells us something about subgrap hs of an
egr(v, k, g, λ ) graph. The lemma and its proof essentially belong to [8, Th. 2.3],
but we have slightly altered it to better highlight the results that we n eed.

